WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.819 align:start position:0%
 
Steve Jobs once echoed a quote  originally attributed to Henry Ford that  said if I had asked people what they  wanted they would have said faster  horses the implication being the  consumers don't know what they want and  it's the job of entrepreneurs to  anticipate people's needs such as horses  being replaced by cars but the Internet  has changed things we're now living in a  world where consumers are clued in wise  to technology and deeply aware of what  they want in the realm of gaming  inviting player feedback can bring about  a better product or simply earn goodwill  and show transparency to a loyal player  base a currency that's worth a lot  especially for ongoing games that hope  to retain their players game development  is rarely a trouble-free endeavor and as  with the majority of sizeable online  games the division too has run into its  share of bugs and balancing issues since  launch but massive entertainments  transparency around these issues is  impressive constantly evolving online  games are often doomed to be in an  endless cycle of rebalancing bug fixing  and maintenance while there's an  advantage in the adaptability such a  model offers the constant flux and  change ability can prove to be a  double-edged sword one issue is that  players expect constant change  improvements and new features I got in  touch with massive entertainment  community developers Peter Martinson and  christophe gans left to find out how  they engage with and incorporate player  feedback they said we want our community  to know that we see the development of  the division to as an ongoing  relationship that said we and the  players don't always agree on everything  we need to constantly have a holistic  view of what goes in and what goes out  of the game but we discussed the  feedback we get on a daily basis when it  comes to what can and can't be  implemented they explained our designers  and production teams know the game  intimately  they know what's feasible and what's not  feasible and they know how certain  changes that are being requested would  or wouldn't work practically sometimes  feedback can be laser focused on a  specific aspect of the game that if  changed could have much bigger  implications that you don't directly see  at the same time many of our players  also know the game very well so nothing  is discarded Martinson and Gansler said  keeping up with the development demands  of a constantly  evolving game can be hard though very  rewarding the division to released as  new reports of intense development  crunch popped up within the industry  epic employees have described unending  and brutal crunch periods while working  on fortnight a demand of the ongoing  nature of the game the practice of  crunch which requires extraordinarily  long hours is becoming a particularly  publicized issue across the industry I  asked her massive viewed crunch culture  and they said when it comes to  developing online evolving games or  games as a service there's less ramp up  and ramp downtime than during  traditional game production we're  actively working to prevent teams  working overtime and when it does happen  we have very clear rules and  communication about the practice for  example overtime has always paid in  accordance with Swedish work regulations  work-life balance is essential if we  want to attract and retain the best  talent to continue developing games on  the same scale and quality as a division  2 having spoken to the developers I  wanted to ask the division 2 subreddit  community how they felt their  suggestions were being heard and  implemented while it is important to  note that those who replied represent  just a small sampling of the community  there were some interesting shared  sentiments over all the players who  responded said massive is communicative  and transparent citing examples like  when the community asked for skill and  weapon changes at launch and those  adjustments were implemented very  quickly several plays noted that  sometimes this responsiveness turns into  over responsiveness and that things have  changed as a result of player feedback  that don't necessarily please everybody  an example of this is the original plan  to increase the gear score which  essentially functions as the game's  level cap from 500 to 515 this didn't  end up happening after some player said  they didn't want the score increased and  massive ultimately decided it was too  early to introduce higher gear score  items given everything else that changed  in title update 3 but the community is  very divided on whether this is a good  or a bad call on the other side of the  coin a number of players myself included  were not a fan of debuffs on weapon mods  and a massive made the call to remove  them there  thousands of threads YouTube videos  forum posts and tweets out there from  incredibly clued in Division two players  reporting back with what they want to  see change in the game  many of these raise criticisms of  ongoing bugs loot drop issues and enemy  AI balancing among many many other  requests this is something massive seem  to be taking pretty seriously they  explained sometimes things go wrong and  we don't believe in hiding from our  mistakes we're human and mistakes happen  transparency is very important for us  it's part of the DNA of the project of  course we can't talk about everything  even if we'd want to that's just the  reality of the game's design and the  business this relates to the most  interesting point of praise that the  players I spoke to mentioned they said  that even if they didn't like the  implementation of a certain change they  appreciated being included in the  process there is an undercurrent of  dissatisfaction in the division to play  a base right now but I do wonder if the  reason players keep vocalizing their  criticisms is that they have an  expectation that they're being heard and  their opinions are not falling on deaf  ears in just the last week one of the  highest upvoted subreddit posts  simply read when there are issues with  the game most of the time the devs are  silent but it is nice to know that the  devs care and are talking about the  issues weekly this is in the midst of  dozens of suggestions about what could  and should be changed but the fact that  both sentiments coexist is important to  many players feeling heard and respected  and knowing that the game they throw  hundreds of hours into is developed by  people who care what they have to say  and open about their successes and  failures is more important than having  every suggestion they want implemented  it's this level of communication and  transparency that results in place  feeling a loyalty to a franchise in a  studio and that's something no amount of  money or marketing can buy  four massive with the division to those  conversations take place through the  regular state of the game live streams  on Twitch through Reddit and Twitter and  YouTube the game's official Thorens a  newly established official discord  server and occasionally in-house  workshops where community members are  invited to speak on the game's  development the emphasis on hearing and  incorporating player feedback isn't just  lip service for massive and the division  - and they aren't the only ones  listening when I was discussing this  video with colleagues and online  communities many of them had an  immediate idea of at least one other  company they thought was doing a great  job at engaging with their community and  incorporating player feedback for  instance a reddit user reached out to  tell me that since launch rare has done  a really good job of keeping the sea of  Thieves community looped in with every  new change  similarly respawn has gone at a  reputation for their great to develop a  player relationship throughout the  development of apex legends the latter  is a particularly interesting example  given respawn has publicly stated  they're choosing to space out content  updates so they can avoid subjecting  employees to crunch the respect and  Trust players give to the studios who  make the games they love seems to go  both ways as Martinson and consolate  told me we know that feedback always  comes from the love of the game so even  if it's something negative players just  want the game to be better we appreciate  that we're at a curious point in the  games industry right now gamers are very  clued in and discerning they aren't  buying marketing and pretty cg trailers  at face value like they once did  game developers from high-profile  studios are speaking up about poor work  conditions and treatment more than ever  the smoke and mirrors that shaded the  magic of game development is fading from  all sides and it's forcing game studios  to change adapt and most importantly  communicate image and honesty are  becoming more and more important for a  studio and ignoring legions of fans when  they're making it quite clear what they  do and do not want can have some fallout  76 sized consequences that is quite  apart from the rippling effects of a  fanbase who feel betrayed all the  consequences of a tarnished reputation  as a result of reports from a workforce  revealing alleged exploitation trust and  communication breakdowns between develop  and players are becoming a disappointing  trend of late so actions that illustrate  respect to both employees and gaming  audiences of being more celebrated than  ever everyone likes to feel heard and  respected and it'll be no surprise to me  if the game studios who are really  listening to their player base and  embodying these values are the ones who  maintain their audiences and in-house  talent

